From 4afa06bfdc6faf293f6d3ede87a445ec76f5fa61 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Thu, 20 Aug 2026 13:06:49 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/6] fix(api): a browser must never replay an answer meant for
 another principal
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Every `/api/` response is principal-specific and none of them said so. With no
`Cache-Control` header the decision fell to the browser's heuristics — and
Chrome will happily store a `410 Gone`, keyed on the URL plus `Vary`
(`Accept, origin`; the credential is not in it).

That is the whole of why the site stopped working for returning visitors. A
guest token expires after 24 h. The next `GET /api/config/` answers 410. Chrome
caches it. From then on *every* `/api/config/` in that browser answers 410 from
cache, whatever token is sent — so the client cleared each fresh session the
moment it created one, uploads landed in sessions nothing could read, and the
workspace showed "An error occurred." A cached 404 for a document does the
same thing in miniature.

So: an error is never stored. A success keeps whatever the view chose — a page
raster pinned to a version is worth caching — and gets `private, no-store` when
the view expressed no opinion.

And the message itself. `Http404` reaches the DRF handler with no `detail` at
all, so every "not yours / already deleted / wrong id" in the product arrived
as the words "An error occurred.", which names nothing and suggests nothing.
Each status now has a sentence a person can act on.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Y58KHCbQzb6yAzQv9a6xbQ
