From 7366225ce128b147511cb5e52a032f32775fac99 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Thu, 20 Aug 2026 13:07:09 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/6] fix(guest): a dead token must not take the live session
 down with it
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Two things went wrong together, and both of them ended at the same bare screen.

Responses do not arrive in the order they were sent. Two `/api/config/` calls
made with a token that had expired overnight both came back 410 — and the
second one, landing *after* the tool page had already minted a fresh session
and uploaded a file into it, cleared the new token. The visitor was left
holding a document that nothing could open. `onSessionExpired` now takes the
credential the failing request actually carried and ignores a verdict about one
it no longer holds.

And a guest whose session has simply rolled over should not have to notice.
`guest_expired` now starts a fresh session and replays the request once, so
somebody coming back the next morning just carries on. The banner is kept for
the case that really lost something: a replay that failed too. One replay,
never a loop — the retry is marked in the request context.

While here: `ensureSession()` shares one mint between every caller that asked
while it was open. Serialising it in the facade is what makes the §21.2
guarantee hold when the callers are not one tool page but a handful of requests
that all came back `guest_expired` at once.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Y58KHCbQzb6yAzQv9a6xbQ
