 | 2026-08-02 | **A stale `base_version_seq` after every save — one race behind several "memory" flakes.** Measured on `main` as well as on this branch (2 of 6 runs red on `main`, in isolation, on a settled stack), so it is pre-existing and not from the retention work. Root cause is in the job row, not the environment: `edit_text` succeeds → `trackReload` fires the toast and calls `viewer.reload()`, which refetches the document *asynchronously*; anything dispatched before that `GET` lands still carries the old `base_version_seq`, and the worker refuses it with `version_conflict` ("The document changed since you loaded it"). Confirmed by reading the failed rows rather than the console: across a full suite run **every** failed job was `version_conflict`, and not only `find_replace` — `page_numbers` and `set_metadata` too, and it takes `phase-3:43` (annotate) down as well as `phase-4:33`. In the product it self-heals — `handleFailure` shows "Document changed — refreshed" and reloads — so this is a real but recoverable annoyance for a user who acts quickly after saving. The fix is to take the new `seq` from the job result the save already returns instead of waiting for the refetch — `trackReload` has the job in hand, but the mode components (`edit`, `forms`, `convert`, `sign`, `annotate`) emit a bare `(saved)` and would each need to carry it. Not bundled here: six feature components, none of which this change touches. | 8 | No | ⬜ |
